  // ---- THE AMBIGUITY TRIPLE (added 2026-07-29 with cross-layer/all-consumes-unjoined) -------------
  // `be-catalog-a` and `be-catalog-b` implement one IDENTICAL three-route API and `fe-catalog` calls it.
  // Adjudicated by reading each anchored line, not by trusting the run:
  //   * duplicate-route x3 PER SOURCE -- both provides.ts files register the same three keys, verified
  //     line by line, and since 2026-07-29 the rule emits ONE COPY PER PROVIDING SOURCE, each anchored in
  //     that source's own tree (the same change db-table-name-in-multiple-sources took above). So `cata`
  //     and `catb` each carry three, and excluding one backend's paths no longer deletes the finding
  //     about the other.
  //   * mutating-route-no-auth on each `POST /catalog/orders` -- neither handler is guarded, and neither
  //     path matches the auth-acquisition vocabulary. Same verdict xbe/provides.ts:8 already carries.
  //   * all-consumes-unjoined at the FIRST of fe-catalog's three calls -- all three keys resolve cleanly
  //     and all three match two provider trees, so the linker emits no edge for any of them. This is the
  //     shape four of the six real dogfood front ends have, and the reason the rule exists.
  // What is NOT here is the point of the fixture: `cross-layer/ambiguous-consume` would have fired once
  // per call site and is REPLACED by the single fold. Disabling all-consumes-unjoined hands those three
  // back, so a regression that breaks the replacement contract shows up as three FPs here.
  "cata/provides.ts:13": ["cross-layer/duplicate-route"],
  "cata/provides.ts:14": ["cross-layer/duplicate-route"],
  "cata/provides.ts:15": ["cross-layer/duplicate-route","mutating-route-no-auth"],
  "catb/provides.ts:7": ["cross-layer/duplicate-route"],
  "catb/provides.ts:8": ["cross-layer/duplicate-route"],
  "catb/provides.ts:9": ["cross-layer/duplicate-route","mutating-route-no-auth"],
  "fecat/consumes.ts:16": ["cross-layer/all-consumes-unjoined"],
