  // ---- THE TWO NEGATIVE-CASE PAIRS (added 2026-07-29) --------------------------------------------
  // Added because this corpus scored TP 143 FN 0 FP 0 -- 100% recall AND 100% precision -- while only 2
  // of 6 front-end trees in the real dogfood corpus joined at all. The cause was that EVERY front-end
  // consume here was a LITERAL PATH: the answer key was planted exclusively INSIDE the capability
  // boundary, so 100% measured the inside of the boundary rather than the capability. Nothing in
  // trees/ contained a cross-file `baseURL` assignment.
  //
  // Two pairs now sit deliberately OUTSIDE it. Each is a front end whose calls carry only the path
  // SUFFIX, paired with a backend that genuinely provides the full route -- so the correct answer is a
  // join and the current answer is a miss:
  //   * feaxios x bearticles  -- `axios.defaults.baseURL = settings.baseApiUrl`, a cross-file constant.
  //     parser/.../adapters/client_base.rs recognizes a string LITERAL only and names this exact
  //     expression as the shape it refuses (never-guess IO convention).
  //   * fegensdk x beinvoices -- a vendored swagger `HttpClient` whose `baseUrl` is read from the
  //     environment. parser/.../adapters/client_base_generated.rs refuses that the same way.
  //
  // BOTH ARE NOW REPAIRED IN THE CONFIG, not in the recognizers (2026-07-29). Neither refusal was ever a
  // bug -- guessing a base mis-keys every consume in the tree -- so the fix was to let the author STATE
  // the base: `trees[].topology.clientBase`, declared on each front end in zzop.config.jsonc. What this
  // fixture measures therefore changed on purpose, from "how big is the hole" to "does the declared
  // repair actually work end to end". The hole itself has not moved: delete either declaration and every
  // expectation below reverts.
  //
  // The declaration also carries the idempotence pin. Each front end ALSO calls the same four routes with
  // full literal paths from server/loader.ts (the Next-style split: a browser client with an implicit
  // base, server-side rendering with `fetch` and no client instance). Those 8 calls join today and must
  // keep joining -- a blind prepend would re-key them to /api/api/... and this gate would report 8 lost
  // joins. See crates/engine/src/analyze/compose/config_client_base.rs, which diverges from the
  // code-extracted pass for exactly this reason.
  //
  // HOW AN UNMET EXPECTATION IS SPELLED HERE. Until 2026-07-29 this harness had exactly two
  // dispositions -- an expectation (absent -> FN) and a `benign` control (present -> FP) -- and no third
  // state for "correct, not yet implemented" (`untracked` annotates a FN's cause but still counts it and
  // still exits nonzero). So these two were written as ORDINARY expectations and the gate was
  // PERMANENTLY RED: red for something nobody intended to fix that week, which blocks every push and
  // teaches everyone to ignore the gate. Writing them as `benign` instead was never an option -- "no
  // finding here is correct" would freeze today's incapacity as the right answer forever, which is the
  // exact reason these cases were added.
  //
  // They lived in the `gap` bucket at the bottom of this file, the third disposition
  // scripts/measure/benchmark.mjs grew that day: excluded from TP/FN/FP, printed as `GAP n/N closed` on
  // its own line in every run, exit-ZERO while open, and exit-NONZERO the day one fires so that progress
  // is promoted by hand rather than absorbed. The coverage floor carries a third column so a gap cannot
  // be deleted silently -- a gap costs nothing in the score, so without that column its removal would be
  // completely invisible.
  //
  // THE TWO `cross-layer/method-mismatch` ENTRIES BELOW ARE THAT PROMOTION, and the mechanism worked
  // exactly as designed: the run that shipped `clientBase` printed `GAP 2/2 closed` and ABORTED, refusing
  // to score until a human moved these two lines out of `gap`. Both backends provide their fourth route
  // as GET only while the front end POSTs to it, so with the base resolved each is a real method mismatch
  // anchored at the consume. Both anchors ALREADY carried `unprovided-mutation-call` (which co-fires with
  // method-mismatch by that rule's own module doc), so the promotion was purely additive.
  //
  // THE `cross-layer/prefix-drift` ENTRIES ARE GONE, and their deletion is the other half of the same
  // hand edit. They were the DEGRADE path and true positives while the base was unresolved: three
  // same-method reads per pair landed as `prefix`-dimension route-near-misses that aggregated into one
  // finding naming the missing `/api`. The declaration resolves the base, the three reads JOIN, and there
  // is no drift left to name. `--update-baseline` grows only, so retiring an expectation is deliberately
  // a hand edit of scripts/detection-expected-baseline.txt -- the ratchet does not let a detection be
  // given up without an author. They got no inverse of `gap` ("will stop firing"), and that was
  // considered and declined the same day: the scorer cannot tell "it vanished because the base resolved"
  // from "it vanished because the near-miss aggregation regressed", so such a marker would convert the
  // ratchet's fail-closed edge into a fail-open one on the recall axis it exists to protect. Adjudicating
  // by hand, once, in the commit that caused it is the cost of that safety -- and this is that commit.
  "feaxios/pages/articles.ts:43": ["cross-layer/unprovided-mutation-call","cross-layer/method-mismatch"],
  "fegensdk/api/Api.ts:21": ["cross-layer/unprovided-mutation-call","cross-layer/method-mismatch"],
  // ---- end negative-case pairs -------------------------------------------------------------------

  "graph/circularA.ts:1": ["circular"],
  "graph/duplicateRoute.ts:6": ["cross-layer/unconsumed-endpoint"],
  "graph/duplicateRoute.ts:7": ["cross-layer/unconsumed-endpoint","duplicate-route"],
  "graph/routeShadowing.ts:10": ["cross-layer/unconsumed-endpoint"],
  "graph/routeShadowing.ts:11": ["cross-layer/unconsumed-endpoint","route-shadowing"],
  "island/deadA.ts:1": ["circular","unreachable"],
  "island/deadB.ts:1": ["unreachable"],
  "island/orphan.ts:1": ["unreachable"],
  // ---- THE JAVA LANE OF FOUR MULTI-LANGUAGE RULES (added 2026-07-29) -----------------------------
  // Fifteen `security` rules admit `.java`. Eleven had a Java fixture (UnsafeController/UserRepository
  // below); these four admitted `.java` in their file_pattern but were only ever exercised on TypeScript,
  // so the Java half of each regex shipped unmeasured. Adjudicated by reading each anchored line — every
  // one sits on a planted defect whose `good` counterpart is the next line and correctly does NOT fire.
  //   * :14 a secret-named field assigned a literal (the next line reads System.getenv instead)
  //   * :19 user:password inside a postgresql:// URI
  //   * :24 an api_key travelling as a query parameter
  //   * :29 MD5 over a password — weak-crypto CO-FIRES here and that is correct, not a duplicate: MD5 is
  //     both a weak digest and the wrong choice for passwords, and the two rules say different things.
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/CredentialsConfig.java:14": ["security/hardcoded-secret"],
